XR presentation: stereoscopic 3D, the frame encoded again for the right eye (Android)

While the XR layer has turned it on (aurora_xr_set_stereo, include/aurora/xr.h) the XR buffers are
two eyes wide. Each frame's left eye is encoded as it is recorded; at the end of the frame
(gfx::end_frame, on the render worker, before the packet is released) the EFB is copied aside and
every op of the frame packet is encoded again for the right eye (gfx/stereo.cpp, encoding.cpp
stereo::encode_right_eye). EFB copies and whatever samples the screen are thus redone with each eye's
picture, with no second set of textures. The replay skips what is for the CPU (encoder tasks with an
after-submit step, the depth snapshot) and GPU timing; staging copies and uploads were already
encoded.

The shift: perspective draws in a pass on the EFB whose viewport covers (nearly) all of it carry the
eye's shift and focus in DrawImmediateData::_pad (two snorm16), and the GX vertex shader moves them
in clip space: clip.x += eye * strength * (clip.w - focus), only when the projection is a perspective
one. Orthographic (2D, HUD) and partial-viewport draws (shadows, reflections) are drawn as recorded.
The census, which uses _pad itself, turns the shift off. The pipeline cache holds configurations, not
shaders, so it needs no new version.

The present puts the left eye's copy in the buffer's left half and the EFB in the right half, and
tells the XR layer (AuroraXrFrame.sideBySide, .stereo). A frame with ImGui drawn over it (the options
menu) is not replayed: it is shown flat, in the left half; imgui::freeze returns an empty DrawData
when nothing is drawn so the present can tell.

